{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically above realized prices, with the bias growing with horizon (small in week 1, larger in weeks 2–4). Apply a horizon-dependent downward adjustment (increasing with lead time).",
    "Over-optimistic rebounds: after drawdowns, the model predicts stronger/longer rebounds than occur. Damp positive day-to-day changes (shrink forecasted up-moves), especially from ~day 8 onward.",
    "Insufficient downside momentum: during sustained sell-offs the model declines too slowly (lags the downtrend). Add a trend-following correction when the last 5–10 days are negative (pull the path down faster).",
    "Overshoot on local peaks: when the model projects a mid-horizon peak (around days 10–18), the true series peaks lower and rolls over earlier. Cap peak height and shift the turning point earlier by a few days.",
    "Too-high long-run level: by the last third of the horizon the model tends to converge to a level ~5–10 EUR/t above realized. Anchor the tail (days 20–30) closer to recent realized lows/rolling mean rather than the model’s higher terminal level."
  ]
}
